Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    Update NEWS for 0.13.0-mlg
    
    Summarize the changes since the 0.12.21-mlg tag: the conversion from
    the lrz/lsz programs to a library, the API behaviour changes and the
    memory safety fixes.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    Bump version to 0.13.0-mlg and libtool version to 1:0:0
    
    The package version moves from 0.12.21-mlg to 0.13.0-mlg.  The shared
    library had no -version-info (0:0:0); since the public interface has
    changed incompatibly (the zmodem_send tick callback) and grown
    (zmodem_last_result), set it to 1:0:0.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zmodem_receive, zmodem_send: enable timeouts
    
    Both entry points created their state with timeouts disabled, so a
    peer that never answered blocked the caller forever.  Use the
    protocol's normal timeouts: with no peer at all the sender gives up
    after about 30 seconds and the receiver after about 160 seconds, and
    the session is reported as failed.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zreadline: restore the caller's SIGALRM handler after a timed read
    
    Every timed read installed the library's SIGALRM handler and left it
    in place, permanently replacing any handler the calling program had
    set.  Put the previous handler back once the read returns.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    report session failure through zmodem_last_result() and the exit status
    
    Since zmodem_receive()/zmodem_send() return a byte count, a caller
    could not tell a failed session from one that transferred nothing,
    and mrz/msz always exited 0.  Add zmodem_last_result() and make both
    tools exit 1 when the session failed (previously 128, or 1 for
    unreadable files, from the library's own exit()).

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: report path security violations at error level
    
    These messages were logged at info level, below the level the library
    runs at, so a rejected file name ended the session with no visible
    reason now that the generic fatal message from bibi() is gone.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    return to the caller on fatal errors instead of exiting
    
    Out-of-memory, TCP setup failures and the receiver's path security
    violation still called exit() from inside the library, killing the
    calling program (the latter on a file name chosen by the sender).
    These sites now call zm_fatal(), which longjmp()s back to
    zmodem_receive()/zmodem_send(); those restore the terminal, release
    the session and return.  The misnamed bibi() in lrz.c is no longer
    needed.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    mrz: add -d DIR to choose the receive directory
    
    zmodem_receive() takes a directory but mrz always passed NULL.  Add a
    -d option; without it files still go to the current directory.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: only remove the file that was being received when a session fails
    
    On a session error the receiver unlinked rz->pathname unconditionally.
    When no file was open that is the name of the last file handled, so a
    cancel between files deleted a file that had just been received
    completely (or an existing file that had been skipped).  Remove it
    only if it was still open for writing.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: reject absolute pathnames when there is no approver
    
    zmodem.h documents that with a NULL APPROVER files with absolute
    pathnames are rejected, but they were accepted, letting the sender
    choose any writable location.  Skip such files.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: stop the transfer when the tick callback returns false
    
    zmodem.h documents that a false return from TICK terminates the
    receive, but the receiver ignored the return value.  Abort the session
    as the sender already does.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zmodem_receive: honour the directory argument
    
    The directory argument was ignored and files always went to the
    current working directory.  Map relative received names below the
    given directory for every local file operation (existence check,
    rename probing, open, and the later utime/chmod/unlink through
    rz->pathname).  Callbacks still see the name as sent.  With a NULL
    directory nothing changes.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zmodem_receive, zmodem_send: honour the min_bps argument
    
    min_bps was accepted but a hard-coded 0 was passed down, so the
    minimum-rate check was never enabled.  Pass it through, converting
    the documented bits per second to the bytes per second used
    internally, with the receiver's existing 120 second grace period on
    both sides (the sender had 0, which would abort on the second slow
    sample).

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zmodem_receive, zmodem_send: return instead of calling exit()
    
    Both entry points ended with exit(), so the caller never regained
    control and the documented return value was never produced.  Restore
    the terminal, release the session state and return the sum of the
    sizes of the files transferred successfully.
    
    Fatal internal errors (out of memory, a path security violation in
    the receiver) still terminate the process as before.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    mrz, msz: implement the -q (quiet) option
    
    -q was listed in both getopt strings (in msz wrongly as taking an
    argument) but had no case, so it fell into the default branch and
    called abort().  Make it suppress the progress and per-file reports on
    stderr; error messages are still printed.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    make the alarm() timeouts actually interrupt blocking calls
    
    The SIGALRM handlers were installed with signal(), which on glibc sets
    SA_RESTART, so read() (and accept()) simply resumed after the alarm
    and the timeout never fired.  Install them with sigaction() without
    SA_RESTART in zreadline.c and tcp.c.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: report the received size to the complete callback
    
    The receiver passed zi->bytes_sent, which is never set on the
    receiving side, so the callback always saw a size of 0.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lsz: use %zu to log the size_t block length
    
    sz->blklen is a size_t but was formatted with %d.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    check the result of malloc/calloc before using it
    
    Several allocations (the zm, zreadline, rz and sz state structures,
    the intro message buffer, the rename buffer, msz's file list) were
    written to without a NULL check.  Fail with "out of memory" like the
    other allocations in the library already do.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zreadline: use %zu to log the size_t read size
    
    readline_readnum is a size_t but was formatted with %d, which is
    undefined and misreads the variadic arguments on LP64.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    rbsb: pass an int to ioctl(FIONREAD)
    
    FIONREAD stores an int, but rdchk() passed the address of a long, so
    only part of the variable was written (the high half on big-endian
    LP64, making rdchk() always return 0).  It was also static, so a
    failed ioctl returned the previous call's count; return 0 instead.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    tcp: verify the resolved address fits in sin_addr
    
    tcp_connect() copied h_length bytes from gethostbyname() into a 4-byte
    IPv4 address without checking the address family or length.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    tcp: use socklen_t for socket address lengths
    
    getsockname() and accept() were given a size_t * where socklen_t * is
    required.  On LP64 these have different sizes, so the kernel read and
    wrote only half of the variable (the wrong half on big-endian).

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lsz: take the file list as const char ** in sz_transmit_files
    
    zmodem_send() passed its const char ** file list to a char *[]
    parameter, an incompatible pointer conversion that newer compilers
    reject.  The names are never modified.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zmodem_send: declare the tick callback with the arguments it is called with
    
    The public prototype declared a five-argument tick callback, but the
    sender stores and calls it as the six-argument form (with the file
    name first) used by zmodem_receive() and by msz.  A callback written
    to the header would be called through an incompatible type and receive
    shifted arguments.  Make the declaration match the call.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lsz: check the temporary file name length in sz_send_pseudo
    
    TMPDIR/TMP and the pseudo file name were copied into a PATH_MAX+1
    buffer with memcpy()/sprintf() without any length check, so a long
    TMPDIR overflowed the heap block.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lsz: fix use after free in sz_send_pseudo error message
    
    The temporary file name was freed just before being printed in the
    "cannot open tmpfile" message.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lsz: bound copies into the local file name buffer
    
    sz_transmit_file() copied the ONAME environment variable (and the
    caller's file name) into a PATH_MAX+1 stack buffer with strcpy().
    An over-long ONAME overflowed the stack; truncate instead.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lsz: initialize struct stat in sz_transmit_pathname
    
    fstat() is skipped for stdin and for the empty terminating pathname,
    but f.st_size was still used to update totalleft and to fill bytes
    126/127 of the header block, sending uninitialized stack contents.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lsz: detect read errors in sz_filbuf
    
    The result of read() was stored in a size_t, so -1 became SIZE_MAX,
    passed the '<= 0' test and the uninitialized buffer was sent as a
    full block, repeatedly, for as long as the error persisted.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lsz: clear the input FILE pointer after closing it
    
    sz_getinsync() closes input_f on ZSKIP/ZRINIT, and its callers then
    tested the stale pointer and closed it again (double fclose whenever
    the file is not mmap()ed, e.g. an empty file that the receiver skips).
    Set input_f to NULL wherever it is closed, and guard the two places
    that could then be reached with no open file.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lsz: do not read past the mapped file when ZRPOS is beyond EOF
    
    The send offset is taken from the receiver's ZRPOS without checking it
    against the file size.  For an offset past EOF, 'mm_size - bytes_sent'
    wrapped to a huge length and the sender read (and transmitted) memory
    beyond the mmap()ed file.  Send an empty final subpacket instead.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: discard saved out-of-sync blocks when a new file starts
    
    Blocks saved beyond the final position of a file stayed on the global
    list, leaking them and letting them be written into the next file
    when its offset happened to match.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: do not save empty out-of-sync blocks
    
    A saved block is only dropped once the file position has moved past
    it.  A zero-length block never advances the position, so replaying it
    looped forever at 'goto nxthdr'.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: fix endless loop on pause/break codes in the Attn string
    
    After handling a \335 (break) or \336 (pause) byte the loop advanced
    p but not s, so it found the same byte again forever.  The Attn string
    is supplied by the sender in ZSINIT, so a peer could hang the receiver.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: fix buffer underflow on an empty file name
    
    For a header with an empty name and no info string, the 'zap trailing
    period' code read and possibly wrote the byte before the start of the
    heap-allocated name.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: log the rejected file name, not the previous pathname
    
    The approver-rejection message printed rz->pathname, which at that
    point is NULL for the first file (NULL passed to %s) or the name of
    the previously received file.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: size the pathname buffer from the received file name
    
    pathname was a fixed 2*PATH_MAX (8192) byte buffer filled with strcpy/
    sprintf, but the name comes from an up to 8192 byte header (plus a
    ".NNN" rename suffix), so a long name overflowed the heap block.
    Allocate exactly what is needed.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: stop when rz_process_header reports ZFERR
    
    On a failed resume (CRC check error or fseek failure) the header code
    closes the output file and returns ZFERR, but callers only tested for
    ERROR and went on to receive data into the closed stream.  Treat ZFERR
    as a fatal file error, as is done when closing the file fails.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: close the existing file when a transfer is skipped
    
    The newer / newer-or-longer / CRC management checks open the existing
    file for reading and returned without closing it when the file was
    skipped, leaking one FILE (and descriptor) per skipped file.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: clear the output FILE pointer after closing it
    
    rz->fout was left pointing at a closed stream.  After a file completed
    (or was skipped because it exists) and the session then failed, the
    error path in rz_receive() saw a non-NULL fout and called fclose() on
    it a second time.  Set fout to NULL wherever it is closed.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: fix double free and dangling pointers to the saved file name
    
    rz_process_header() frees its static name copy on entry but returned
    early (file name ending in '/') without clearing the pointer, so the
    next call freed it again.  With nflag set the copy was also replaced
    while zi->fname still pointed at the freed block, which is later
    handed to the tick and complete callbacks.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: initialize file info fields before parsing the header
    
    sscanf() does not assign fields it fails to parse, so a malformed or
    short info string left bytes_total, modtime and mode uninitialized
    and they were then used as the file size, time and mode.  Start from
    the same defaults used when no info string is present.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: NUL-terminate the received file name header
    
    The ZFILE/YMODEM header in secbuf is parsed with strlen() and sscanf()
    as "name\0info\0", but nothing terminated it.  A header without NULs
    made the parser run into stale data from earlier blocks or past the
    end of secbuf.  Append two NULs after the received bytes.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: fix %s format used with an int in bibi()
    
    bibi() formatted its integer argument with %s, dereferencing it as a
    pointer.  It is reached from rz_checkpath() on a path security
    violation, so a rejected filename crashed instead of exiting cleanly.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zm: bound the retry loops in zm_do_crc_check
    
    The t1/t2 retry counters were never incremented, so a peer that never
    answers the ZCRC request (timeouts or garbage headers) kept the
    receiver in this loop forever.  Count the retries as evidently
    intended: three ZCRC requests, three headers read for each.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zm: truncate local CRC to 32 bits in zm_do_crc_check
    
    'crc = ~crc' on a 64-bit unsigned long sets the upper 32 bits, so the
    comparison against the 32-bit CRC from the peer could never match and
    every file was reported as ZCRC_DIFFERS on LP64 systems.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zm: do not store length+1 bytes when a data subpacket is too long
    
    zm_receive_data() and zm_read_data32() wrote one byte past the given
    length before reporting "Data subpacket too long".  For the ZSINIT
    attention string this overwrote the terminating NUL of attn[], which
    is later used as a C string.  The extra byte is now rejected without
    being stored; return values are unchanged.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zm: fix leak of intro message buffer on overlong intro
    
    zm_get_header() returned without freeing intro_msg when the intro
    message length was exceeded.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zm: fix off-by-one that left the intro message unterminated
    
    The length check lets max_intro_msg_len + 1 bytes be stored in a buffer
    of exactly that size, overwriting the terminating NUL, so logging the
    message read past the end of the heap block.  Allocate one more byte.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zm: do not use the received intro message as a format string
    
    zm_get_header() passed bytes received from the peer directly to
    log_info() as the format, so a '%s' or '%n' sent before a header could
    crash or corrupt the process.  Log it through "%s".

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    zm: fix out-of-bounds read of frametypes[] for unknown frame types
    
    frametypes[] has 21 non-negative entries (types 0..20) but the range
    check allowed c <= 22, so a peer sending frame type 21 or 22 made
    zm_get_header() read past the end of the array and pass the result to
    a %s format.  Derive the limit from the array size instead.

Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>	2026-11-25

    lrz: remove duplicate definition of program_name
    
    program_name was defined in both lrz.c and lsz.c, so the library fails to
    link with -fno-common (the GCC 10+ default).  Keep the initialized
    definition in lsz.c; lrz.c uses the extern declaration in zglobal.h.
    Previously the two were merged, so the value seen by lrz is unchanged.

